<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/13th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Campaign</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mongol invasion of Europe</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1236">1236</a>–<a href="/years/1242">1242</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Mongol invasions of Europe</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mongol invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> was the campaign of 1236–1242 in which Mongol armies under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batu-khan">Batu Khan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subutai">Subutai</a> conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgars</a> and the principalities of Rus' and then invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. They won at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legnica">Legnica</a> and Mohi in April 1241, but withdrew in 1242, probably after learning of the death of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-khan">Great Khan</a> Ögedei.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mongol-invasion-of-europe.jpg" alt="Mongol invasion of Europe" width="320" height="188" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Qiushufang · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1236-1242_Mongol_invasions_of_Europe.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The operations were planned by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subutai">Subutai</a> and commanded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batu-khan">Batu Khan</a> and Kadan, grandsons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>. The army was ethnically mixed: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> formed the leadership and elite cavalry, but some historians estimate that 70 to 80 percent of the soldiers were Turkic nomads such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumans">Cumans</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bashkirs">Bashkirs</a> and Volga Bulgars, whom European sources called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatars</a>. In 1223 the Mongols had routed a Rus' army of nearly 50,000 at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-kalka-river">Battle of the Kalka River</a> before turning back for almost a decade.</p><p>Ögedei Khan ordered Batu to conquer Rus' in 1235. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> was divided. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> had preached a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a> in 1238, and Emperor Frederick II was excommunicated four times by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-ix">Pope Gregory IX</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-innocent-iv">Pope Innocent IV</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ryazan">Ryazan</a> in December 1237, sacked it and stormed Suzdalia. Grand Prince Yuri was killed on the Sit River on 4 March 1238, and Vladimir, Torzhok and Kozelsk fell. They then crushed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kipchaks">Kipchaks</a> and Alans, and in 1239 sacked Pereyaslavl and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chernigov">Chernigov</a>. Kiev was sacked on 6 December 1240.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-europe">Central Europe</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> advanced with three armies. One defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-the-pious">Henry II the Pious</a>, Duke of Silesia, and his allies at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-legnica">Battle of Legnica</a> on 9 April 1241. Another crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carpathian-mountains">Carpathians</a> and a third followed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>. They regrouped on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sajo">Sajó</a> river and defeated King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bela-iv-of-hungary">Béla IV of Hungary</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mohi">Battle of Mohi</a> on 11 April 1241. The invasion is said to have killed half of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>'s population. Detachments raided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meissen">Meissen</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wiener-neustadt">Wiener Neustadt</a>, and Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii">Frederick II</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>'s forces defeated a small Mongol squadron near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>. Béla had received Batu's demand for surrender in 1237 and had not replied.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>In late March 1242 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> began to withdraw. The most common explanation is the death of the Great Khan Ögedei on 11 December 1241, which called the princes home to elect a successor, and Giovanni da Pian del Carpine reported this reason. Stephen Pow has pointed out that a messenger would have had to cover the distance very fast in winter. Other explanations are the exhaustion of pasture on the Hungarian plain, wet weather that muddied the ground and weakened the Mongol bows, and stone fortresses such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esztergom">Esztergom</a> that made a war of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attrition">attrition</a> hard to win.</p><p>Batu retreated to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>, and the next <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurultai">kurultai</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a> was not held until 1246. Much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a> came under the Horde's rule, and raids continued into the late 13th century. After 1241 Béla IV built stone castles and reformed his army, and when Nogai Khan and Talabuga attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> in 1285 the invasion was repelled.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mongol invasion of Europe” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
